## Why GPL

potrace is GPL-2.0-or-later, and every browser WebAssembly port of it inherits that.
Because vectrace ships potrace to the user's browser as part of a single combined work,
the whole application must be GPL-2.0-or-later.

The MIT/Apache-2.0 dependencies are compatible with this: for the dual-licensed Rust
crates we elect the MIT arm, which is GPL-2.0 compatible (Apache-2.0 alone is not
compatible with GPL-2.0-only, though it is with the "or later" upgrade path).

`flo_curves` is Apache-2.0 only. This is fine under GPL-2.0-**or-later**, since the
combined work can be distributed under GPL-3.0, with which Apache-2.0 is compatible.

Source is available at the project repository, satisfying GPL §3.

## If potrace is dropped

vtracer 1.0 has its own binary mode with Bradley-Roth adaptive thresholding
(`binary_adaptive`, `binary_adaptive_window`, `binary_adaptive_t`) and a `Bw` preset.
If that proves good enough and the potrace engine is removed, every remaining dependency
is MIT or Apache-2.0 and the project can be relicensed to MIT.
